                <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">What exactly is done in bariatric surgery?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">In bariatric surgery, the surgeon modifies the structure of the stomach and sometimes the small intestine to restrict how much food you can eat, reduce nutrient absorption, and alter gut hormones. The most common procedures are sleeve gastrectomy (removing 75–80% of the stomach) and gastric bypass (creating a small stomach pouch and rerouting the intestine). All modern bariatric procedures are performed laparoscopically through tiny keyhole incisions under general anesthesia.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">How long does bariatric surgery take?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">The operating time varies depending on the type of procedure and the patient's anatomy. A laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy typically takes about 60 to 90 minutes. A laparoscopic gastric bypass usually takes 90 to 150 minutes. More complex procedures like BPD/DS can take 2 to 3 hours or longer. The patient is under general anesthesia for the entire duration and wakes up in the recovery room shortly after the surgery is complete.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Is bariatric surgery painful?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">During the surgery, you are completely unconscious under general anesthesia and feel nothing at all. After waking up, you may feel some soreness and discomfort around the small incision sites. However, since the surgery is done laparoscopically (through tiny cuts), post-operative pain is significantly lower than traditional open surgery. Most patients describe the discomfort as manageable with prescribed pain medications and report feeling much better within a few days.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">How much weight can I lose after bariatric surgery?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Weight loss results vary depending on the type of surgery, your starting BMI, and your commitment to lifestyle changes. On average, patients lose 60% to 80% of their excess body weight within 12 to 18 months. Gastric bypass and BPD/DS tend to produce greater total weight loss than sleeve gastrectomy or gastric band. Maintaining the results long-term depends on diet, physical activity, and regular follow-up with your medical team.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Can bariatric surgery be done more than once?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Yes, in certain cases. If a patient has not achieved adequate weight loss or has regained significant weight after a previous procedure, a revision or second bariatric surgery may be considered. For example, a gastric band can be converted to a sleeve gastrectomy, or a sleeve can be revised to a gastric bypass. However, revision surgeries are technically more complex and carry higher risks. Your surgeon will assess whether a revision is appropriate and safe for your specific situation.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">What is the recovery time after bariatric surgery?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Most patients are discharged from the hospital within 2 to 3 days after surgery. You can typically return to light desk work within 1 to 2 weeks. Physical activities like driving and mild exercise can usually resume after 2 to 4 weeks. Full recovery, including returning to all normal activities and exercise routines, is usually achieved within 4 to 6 weeks. The dietary progression — from liquids to solids — is a gradual process that takes about 6 to 8 weeks in total.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">What are the risks of bariatric surgery?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Like any major surgery, bariatric procedures carry some risks. The most serious short-term risks include anastomotic leaks, internal bleeding, blood clots, and infection. Long-term risks include nutritional deficiencies, acid reflux (especially after sleeve gastrectomy), and in rare cases, weight regain. However, modern bariatric surgery performed by an experienced team has an overall mortality rate of less than 0.1%, making it one of the safest major abdominal surgeries performed today.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Will I need to take supplements after bariatric surgery forever?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Yes, lifelong vitamin and mineral supplementation is essential after bariatric surgery. Because the altered digestive anatomy limits the absorption of certain key nutrients, you will need to take daily supplements including a high-quality multivitamin, calcium citrate, Vitamin D, Vitamin B12, and iron (especially for women). The specific supplement regimen will be customized to your procedure type and monitored regularly through blood tests to prevent deficiency-related complications.</p>
                    </div>
